Serhan Dagtas: Now, let's talk about the 5 different types of protocol templates we have.

399
00:43:02.160 --> 00:43:08.380
Serhan Dagtas: Got biomedical, social behavioral, record review, and two site supplement protocol templates.

400
00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:12.519
Serhan Dagtas: Let's start with the bio-medical template.

401
00:43:12.910 --> 00:43:23.619
Serhan Dagtas: Now, you will use this biomedical template for biomedical research, and this includes investigational or biological devices, drugs, experimental procedures.

402
00:43:26.480 --> 00:43:35.710
Serhan Dagtas: You will use the Social Behavioral Research Profile Template if you are doing behavioral observations, focus groups, interviews.

403
00:43:39.010 --> 00:43:46.859
Serhan Dagtas: You will use a record review protocol template when you're doing a retrospective chart review or medical data analysis.

404
00:43:49.860 --> 00:44:02.920
Serhan Dagtas: Now let's talk about the site supplement protocol template. As I mentioned, there are two of these. There's one for industry-sponsored biomedical research and another for industry-sponsored social behavioral research.

405
00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:07.710
Serhan Dagtas: So, what I want you to do is look at the sponsor protocol.

406
00:44:07.710 --> 00:44:20.069
Serhan Dagtas: And our template, and if at any section within our template, if we are requesting information that's already in the sponsor protocol.

407
00:44:20.070 --> 00:44:31.429
Serhan Dagtas: Instead of typing the information within our template, you're just going to give us the page number that corresponds with that information. We don't want you typing the information twice.

408
00:44:31.540 --> 00:44:32.360
Serhan Dagtas: So…

409
00:44:32.810 --> 00:44:47.930
Serhan Dagtas: For example, if you're asking for inclusion and exclusion criteria, and it's already within the sponsor's protocol, just give us the page number in the USF Template section for the inclusion and exclusion criteria.

410
00:44:48.220 --> 00:45:01.069
Serhan Dagtas: Okay, what we are going to do is, we'll do the introductory, IRB introduction first. This goes through some templates and the application process. We are going to actually cover this within the context of

411
00:45:01.180 --> 00:45:12.739
Serhan Dagtas: UILR's processes. Guys, this is fantastic. So I'm just going to switch back to the introductory video instead of going through the

412
00:45:12.850 --> 00:45:15.670
Serhan Dagtas: Institution specific

413
00:45:15.790 --> 00:45:19.379
Serhan Dagtas: Templates here and we're going to discuss some of these.

414
00:45:19.590 --> 00:45:30.890
Serhan Dagtas: My name is Dr. UALR's processes in the second part of the class. Dr. Titania Harris, and it is my pleasure to present the USF IRB Lecture Series.

415
00:45:31.180 --> 00:45:41.160
Serhan Dagtas: These presentations are being made available to provide faculty and students with education on common USF IRB and Pulse IRB topics.

416
00:45:41.330 --> 00:45:47.740
Serhan Dagtas: We have three lecture presentations and this lecture is IRB 101.

417
00:45:48.830 --> 00:45:52.690
Serhan Dagtas: IRB 101 covers a brief history of the IRB.

418
00:45:52.810 --> 00:46:03.379
Serhan Dagtas: research ethics, IRB resources, the investigator manual, and then we will end with how to submit an initial study in Bulls IRB.

419
00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:05.700
Serhan Dagtas: Let's get started.

420
00:46:09.010 --> 00:46:16.779
Serhan Dagtas: So this agenda is what we're going to talk about in this lecture series. First things first, what is an IRB?

421
00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:19.229
Serhan Dagtas: Why do we have an IRB?

422
00:46:19.580 --> 00:46:23.040
Serhan Dagtas: Research ethics using the Belmont Report.

423
00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:24.710
Serhan Dagtas: Types of review.

424
00:46:25.150 --> 00:46:35.159
Serhan Dagtas: IRB resources, investigator manual, and like I said, we're going to end with how to submit an initial study in MOLS IRB.

425
00:46:40.340 --> 00:46:46.190
Serhan Dagtas: We're going to start with this initial question. What is an IRB?

426
00:46:48.660 --> 00:46:58.700
Serhan Dagtas: So, IRB is an acronym for Institutional Review Board. IRB is responsible for the review and approval of all research involving human subjects.

427
00:46:58.950 --> 00:47:09.300
Serhan Dagtas: The function of the IRB is protecting the rights, welfare, and safety of human subjects, and we achieve this through review, approval, and continuing oversight.

428
00:47:15.580 --> 00:47:19.250
Serhan Dagtas: Next, we will ask, why do we have an IRB?

429
00:47:19.720 --> 00:47:28.769
Serhan Dagtas: Now, this is a question that we are often asked by frustrated PIs and study team members. Why do we even have a study?

430
00:47:29.090 --> 00:47:30.489
Serhan Dagtas: Let me tell you why.

431
00:47:32.910 --> 00:47:45.079
Serhan Dagtas: So the birth of the Institutional Review Board, or IRP, resulted from an unfortunate history of crimes against humanity in the name of science and research.

432
00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:50.430
Serhan Dagtas: Let's talk about the number of trials.

433
00:47:50.550 --> 00:47:59.400
Serhan Dagtas: The Nuremberg Trials is a series of military tribunals that was in response to World War II atrocities that happened in concentration camps.

434
00:47:59.660 --> 00:48:02.890
Serhan Dagtas: Researchers, and I use that term loosely.

435
00:48:03.000 --> 00:48:09.419
Serhan Dagtas: Conducted cruel experiments on children and adults that were held in these camps, and didn't offer informed consent.

436
00:48:09.810 --> 00:48:21.689
Serhan Dagtas: Now, these researchers defended their experiments, saying they were morally justified, because the participants were going to die anyway, and sacrifice would provide scientific knowledge that would benefit many.

437
00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:28.359
Serhan Dagtas: Now, 25 defendants, this is the scary part, 20 of them were medical doctors.

438
00:48:28.680 --> 00:48:33.450
Serhan Dagtas: 15 of the 25 were sentenced… were found guilty.

439
00:48:33.580 --> 00:48:36.340
Serhan Dagtas: And 7 were sentenced to death.

440
00:48:38.310 --> 00:48:44.330
Serhan Dagtas: As a direct result of those experiments, We have the Number code.

441
00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:51.789
Serhan Dagtas: So the Nunfar Code makes clear that the welfare and rights of human subjects must be protected.

442
00:48:52.280 --> 00:48:55.680
Serhan Dagtas: The research conducted must be sound and beneficial.

443
00:48:55.880 --> 00:49:02.889
Serhan Dagtas: And the freedom of human subjects to participate are not as unchallengeable, which means they must volunteer.

444
00:49:05.380 --> 00:49:08.420
Serhan Dagtas: Now let's jump up to the ZIP code study.

445
00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:12.490
Serhan Dagtas: This was from 1932 to 1972, a very long time.

446
00:49:12.600 --> 00:49:15.949
Serhan Dagtas: This study was funded by the federal government.

447
00:49:16.410 --> 00:49:33.819
Serhan Dagtas: Subjects were disadvantaged rural African-American men. Several were already infected, and some were not. Now, the researchers, and I use that term loosely, provided free medical exams, free meals, and burial insurance, but did not tell the participants about their disease.

448
00:49:34.170 --> 00:49:46.069
Serhan Dagtas: They also denied the infected men treatment, even though penicillin was an acceptable treatment in 1943, and specifically available for syphilis in 1952.

449
00:49:47.950 --> 00:49:53.579
Serhan Dagtas: And the compliance goes on and on and on, even down to day 2018.

450
00:49:53.770 --> 00:50:02.659
Serhan Dagtas: And I just recently read that there is another case of noncompliance where researchers obtained

451
00:50:02.910 --> 00:50:19.739
Serhan Dagtas: DNA samples from Indigenous people, and used the DNA sample in another way than what they collected it for. So that's not cool, right? So we have all of this non-compliance that happens, but…

452
00:50:22.900 --> 00:50:28.429
Serhan Dagtas: Non-compliance lets us know where the gaps are in research.

453
00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:33.299
Serhan Dagtas: I told you guys how the Nimburg trials led to the Nimburg Code.

454
00:50:33.730 --> 00:50:42.939
Serhan Dagtas: The Tuskegee Research Noncompliance led to the National Research Act of 1974, which requires regulatory protection for human subjects.

455
00:50:43.410 --> 00:50:55.870
Serhan Dagtas: Now, the National Research Act also created the National Commission for the Protection of Human Subjects of Biomedical and Behavioral Research. Now, this commission wrote the Belmont Report. I'm going to dig into that a little later on.

456
00:50:57.270 --> 00:51:04.780
Serhan Dagtas: In 1981, the DHHS and FDA published convergent regulations that were based on the Belmont Principles.

457
00:51:05.360 --> 00:51:16.889
Serhan Dagtas: In 1991, after 10 years of negotiations, 17 federal departments and agencies agreed to adopt a basic human subjects protection. This is what we refer to today as the Common Rule.

458
00:51:17.200 --> 00:51:32.249
Serhan Dagtas: So, what happens is people do research, they find that there's a gap in how they are conducting the research, and then regulations comes in and fills that gap. The IRB ensures that the gap is filled.

459
00:51:38.340 --> 00:51:43.239
Serhan Dagtas: Now, let's talk about research ethics using the Belmont Report.

460
00:51:43.730 --> 00:51:47.430
Serhan Dagtas: Advil dual action fights pain too. Okay.

461
00:51:48.180 --> 00:51:50.620
Serhan Dagtas: Let's just skip this…

462
00:51:57.840 --> 00:52:06.669
Serhan Dagtas: Now, I told you guys that the National Commission for the Protection of Human Subjects of Biomedical and Behavioral Research issued the Belmont Report in 1979.

463
00:52:06.910 --> 00:52:15.120
Serhan Dagtas: Now, the Bellmont Report summarizes basic ethical principles, the boundaries between research and the routine practice of medicine.

464
00:52:15.370 --> 00:52:21.179
Serhan Dagtas: Assessment of risk-benefit criteria in determining appropriateness of research involving human subjects.

465
00:52:21.560 --> 00:52:26.280
Serhan Dagtas: Appropriate guidelines for the selection of human subjects for participation in research.

466
00:52:26.500 --> 00:52:31.259
Serhan Dagtas: And the nature and definition of informed consent in various research settings.

467
00:52:33.380 --> 00:52:45.309
Serhan Dagtas: Now, it is important to distinguish the difference between biomedical and behavior research, practice of acceptable therapy, and the definition of non-human subjects research.

468
00:52:45.310 --> 00:52:54.179
Serhan Dagtas: Understanding these boundaries supports comprehension in what activities need to undergo review for the protection of human subjects of research.

469
00:52:55.820 --> 00:52:58.689
Serhan Dagtas: Let's talk about the definition of practice.

470
00:52:59.400 --> 00:53:10.560
Serhan Dagtas: Practice refers to interventions that are designed solely to enhance the well-being of an individual patient or client, and that have a reasonable expectation of success.

471
00:53:11.000 --> 00:53:21.300
Serhan Dagtas: I want you to be aware that the purpose of medical and behavioral practice is to provide diagnosis, preventative therapy, and treatment to individuals.

472
00:53:24.410 --> 00:53:44.130
Serhan Dagtas: Research involves a living individual about whom an investigator collects data and or biospecimens and uses, studies, analyzes the data of biospecimens through interaction, intervention, or is identifiable private information that is used, studied, analyzed, generated.

473
00:53:44.250 --> 00:53:49.360
Serhan Dagtas: Now, the key is that the information will be used for generalizable knowledge.

474
00:53:49.510 --> 00:54:02.660
Serhan Dagtas: What is generalizable knowledge? So, generalizable knowledge is information that is produced for the purposes of dissemination to a scientific audience outside of the population served by that institution.

475
00:54:02.770 --> 00:54:05.590
Serhan Dagtas: Basically, it means, are we going to publish it?

476
00:54:08.350 --> 00:54:24.070
Serhan Dagtas: Not human subjects research, or NHSR, involves unidentifiable, de-identified, or coded private information or biological specimens. Investigators cannot readily ascertain the identities of the individuals to whom the data or samples belong.

477
00:54:24.160 --> 00:54:32.379
Serhan Dagtas: Or it can be identifiable information such as a program evaluation or quality improvement project or needs assessment survey.

478
00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:47.740
Serhan Dagtas: NHSR information that is produced will not support generalizable knowledge. And remember, generalizable knowledge is information for the purposes of dissemination to a scientific audience outside of the population served by the institution.

479
00:54:49.270 --> 00:55:04.520
Serhan Dagtas: To support our research community in comprehending NHSR, not human subjects research, we've developed an online survey that will help you determine if your project is not human subjects research or research.

480
00:55:04.520 --> 00:55:19.860
Serhan Dagtas: Now, you can find this survey on the Bulls IRB homepage. The results of your response in the survey will be sent to your email, and please note that if the survey determines that what you're doing is NHSR,

481
00:55:20.070 --> 00:55:39.129
Serhan Dagtas: You have to remove the word research from your documents and cannot have IRB contact information or information about USF IRB overseeing the project. That should be removed also because by definition it is not human subjects research.

482
00:55:43.580 --> 00:55:56.790
Serhan Dagtas: Research and practice may be carried on together when research is designed to evaluate the safety and efficacy of a therapy. Now, the general rule is that if there's any element of research in an activity.

483
00:55:57.120 --> 00:56:02.189
Serhan Dagtas: That activity should undergo review for the protection of human subjects.

484
00:56:07.320 --> 00:56:11.460
Serhan Dagtas: Now, let's talk about the three pillars of the Belmont Report.

485
00:56:12.070 --> 00:56:14.619
Serhan Dagtas: We're going to talk about respect for persons.

486
00:56:14.950 --> 00:56:18.189
Serhan Dagtas: Then you have Beneficence, and then Justice.

487
00:56:20.390 --> 00:56:22.750
Serhan Dagtas: Let's talk about respect for persons.

488
00:56:23.120 --> 00:56:31.139
Serhan Dagtas: So as the two moral requirements, we need to acknowledge autonomy. Individuals should be treated as autonomous agents.

489
00:56:31.290 --> 00:56:37.620
Serhan Dagtas: That means that these individuals have the ability to function independently without control by others.

490
00:56:37.900 --> 00:56:43.009
Serhan Dagtas: You also need to protect those with diminished autonomy, such as your vulnerable populations.

491
00:56:43.400 --> 00:56:50.360
Serhan Dagtas: Respect for persons demands that subjects enter into research voluntarily and with adequate information.

492
00:56:50.550 --> 00:56:55.750
Serhan Dagtas: Informed consent is central to protecting the autonomy of human subjects.

493
00:56:58.050 --> 00:56:59.380
Serhan Dagtas: Beneficence.

494
00:56:59.500 --> 00:57:02.670
Serhan Dagtas: This is in your assessment of risks and benefits.

495
00:57:02.840 --> 00:57:07.299
Serhan Dagtas: Researchers have the obligation to secure the well-being of subjects.

496
00:57:07.510 --> 00:57:16.130
Serhan Dagtas: There's two general rules. Do not harm, and maximize possible benefits while minimizing possible harms.

497
00:57:18.590 --> 00:57:19.770
Serhan Dagtas: Justice.

498
00:57:20.090 --> 00:57:22.869
Serhan Dagtas: This is in the selection of your subjects.

499
00:57:23.210 --> 00:57:28.810
Serhan Dagtas: Researchers should question who receives the benefit of research and who bears its burden.

500
00:57:29.100 --> 00:57:33.909
Serhan Dagtas: There must be fairness in the distribution of the risks and benefits of the research.

501
00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:37.769
Serhan Dagtas: So researchers should ask this question.

502
00:57:38.040 --> 00:57:45.989
Serhan Dagtas: Is the purpose for the treatment justified? Meaning, are the participants easily available or directly related to the problem?

503
00:57:48.440 --> 00:57:53.080
Serhan Dagtas: Now, for additional information on the Belmont Report, you can go to our website.

504
00:57:53.230 --> 00:58:07.699
Serhan Dagtas: irb.research.usf.edu. You're going to click on the Regulation and Guidance tab. Then you're going to click on the Regulatory Links folder, and bam-boom, you'll find the Belmont Report information.

505
00:58:13.130 --> 00:58:19.789
Serhan Dagtas: You may also review our investigator manual or the HHS.gov website.

506
00:58:27.110 --> 00:58:31.589
Serhan Dagtas: Now, let's jump into what are the types of review.

507
00:58:33.500 --> 00:58:45.050
Serhan Dagtas: So, USF IRB supports the PIs and study team members in conducting the research in a way that cultivates innovation and not counter submission processes, research regulations.

508
00:58:45.500 --> 00:58:54.030
Serhan Dagtas: PIs and study team members do not determine the approval of their study. It is determined by the IRB chair and or board.

509
00:58:54.160 --> 00:59:00.689
Serhan Dagtas: Now, let me reiterate, you nor the IRB staff can determine what a study is approved as.

510
00:59:00.800 --> 00:59:11.640
Serhan Dagtas: Exempt and expedited submissions must meet the criteria found in federal regulations and OARC guidance, and the chair is ultimately responsible for determining what type of study you have.

511
00:59:11.940 --> 00:59:19.950
Serhan Dagtas: Now, you can try to predict what approval you will have by clicking on the link at the bottom of this presentation.

512
00:59:20.530 --> 00:59:26.499
Serhan Dagtas: are going to Human Subject Regulations Decisions Chart and following the chart.

513
00:59:34.270 --> 00:59:38.569
Serhan Dagtas: Any questions or comments while we're waiting to skip the.

514
00:59:38.750 --> 00:59:45.539
Serhan Dagtas: So, we will… Go through… Few more points.

515
00:59:46.550 --> 00:59:49.369
Serhan Dagtas: About the IRB process in general.

516
00:59:49.910 --> 00:59:54.589
Serhan Dagtas: Take a break and then we'll talk about the UALR processes afterwards.

517
00:59:55.570 --> 01:00:01.579
Serhan Dagtas: Let's talk about the types of review. We have exempt, expedited, and full board.

518
01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:15.159
Serhan Dagtas: Now, before we jump into the types of review, I want to give you guys some information, some things you need to consider when trying to figure out the timeline for approval.

519
01:00:15.270 --> 01:00:23.460
Serhan Dagtas: And we're going to dig a little deeper into the timeframe of review process a little later on, but there are 3 factors that play into the timeframe.

520
01:00:23.670 --> 01:00:26.569
Serhan Dagtas: Is how well written are your documents?

521
01:00:26.780 --> 01:00:36.440
Serhan Dagtas: The workload of the administrator assigned to your study, and how responsive are you to comments and edits? Again, we're going to dig a little deeper into this a little later on.

522
01:00:36.660 --> 01:00:42.860
Serhan Dagtas: Some other things you need to consider when you're trying to figure out the timeline of approval… is this.

523
01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:54.419
Serhan Dagtas: Minimal risk does not automatically qualify a study for expedited or exempt review. Remember, it must meet the criteria found in the federal regulations and OPART guidance.

524
01:00:54.850 --> 01:01:04.540
Serhan Dagtas: Now, just because your study is exempt or expedited, that doesn't guarantee your study will be reviewed faster. Remember, there are 3 things to take into consideration.

525
01:01:04.760 --> 01:01:13.940
Serhan Dagtas: How well written are your study documents? The workload of the administrator assigned to your study, and how responsible are you to comments and edits?

526
01:01:14.350 --> 01:01:20.740
Serhan Dagtas: Another thing you should take into consideration is your study population and the procedures taking place in your study.

527
01:01:21.320 --> 01:01:34.709
Serhan Dagtas: Does your study involve a vulnerable population that does not allow expedited or exempt review? If so, additional safeguards need to be added in your documentation, and those safeguards need to be reviewed.

528
01:01:36.000 --> 01:01:47.640
Serhan Dagtas: If your study has a survey, does the questions cause emotional stress or harm? If so, additional safeguards need to be added in your study. Those safeguards need to be reviewed.

529
01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:52.600
Serhan Dagtas: Now let's talk about exempt.

530
01:01:53.020 --> 01:01:57.110
Serhan Dagtas: So exempt is minimal risk research. What's minimal risk?

531
01:01:57.230 --> 01:02:02.030
Serhan Dagtas: Minimal risk means that the study risks are the same as the individual pace of their daily life.

532
01:02:02.370 --> 01:02:05.460
Serhan Dagtas: It's going to be reviewed by the chairperson or designee.

533
01:02:05.500 --> 01:02:24.679
Serhan Dagtas: You will receive an IRB approval letter, but then your study is automatically closed in Bull's IRB. Now, let me explain. The Bull's IRB application is closed. Your study is still active. The PI must continue research activities as defined in their approved protocol and consent.

534
01:02:24.950 --> 01:02:43.199
Serhan Dagtas: Now, because your study is closed, you cannot submit modifications or continuing review when you have changes. If you have major changes to the protocol, you're required to submit a new study in Bulls IRP. We're going to dig a little deeper in major and minor changes a little later.

535
01:02:44.410 --> 01:02:49.840
Serhan Dagtas: Examples of exempt would be surveys or interviews, benign behavior interventions.

536
01:02:49.970 --> 01:02:59.150
Serhan Dagtas: Passive observations of public behavior without the collection of identifiers. Analysis of discarded pathological specimens without patient identifiers.

537
01:03:01.190 --> 01:03:03.020
Serhan Dagtas: Let's talk about expedited.

538
01:03:03.500 --> 01:03:10.079
Serhan Dagtas: Expedited, again, is minimal risk. Minimal risk means that the study risks are the same as the individual would face in their daily life.

539
01:03:10.270 --> 01:03:13.290
Serhan Dagtas: Again, it's reviewed by chairperson or designee.

540
01:03:13.700 --> 01:03:16.229
Serhan Dagtas: It has a one-year effective period.

541
01:03:16.590 --> 01:03:18.440
Serhan Dagtas: And a continuing review.

542
01:03:18.630 --> 01:03:24.770
Serhan Dagtas: Now, because of the new common rule, we have two options for expedited continuing reviews.

543
01:03:25.010 --> 01:03:34.750
Serhan Dagtas: The first option is the regular way that you do, you say traditional. When you log into Bulls IRB, you complete that entire continuing review application.

544
01:03:35.880 --> 01:03:43.170
Serhan Dagtas: The second one is where you go into Bull's RV, you answer about two questions, and you confirm ongoing research.

545
01:03:43.350 --> 01:03:51.169
Serhan Dagtas: Now, I want to make you aware that you or the IRB can decide on the type of pitching room you have.

546
01:03:51.310 --> 01:03:54.410
Serhan Dagtas: It is based on the content of your study.

547
01:03:55.000 --> 01:04:06.970
Serhan Dagtas: You should review the IRB approval letters that you receive, because those letters will give you specific information about your effective period, as well as the continuing review that you may have.

548
01:04:07.750 --> 01:04:14.550
Serhan Dagtas: Examples of expedited would be collection of data from voice, video, digital, image recordings for research purposes.

549
01:04:14.710 --> 01:04:20.870
Serhan Dagtas: Collection of data through non-invasive procedures routinely employed in clinical practice, such as an MRI.

550
01:04:23.620 --> 01:04:25.520
Serhan Dagtas: Now let's jump to the full board.

551
01:04:25.650 --> 01:04:28.980
Serhan Dagtas: Full board is greater than minimal risk studies.

552
01:04:29.970 --> 01:04:36.570
Serhan Dagtas: It can also be minimal risk studies, like the exempt and expedited, if they don't fall into the exempt and expedited category.

553
01:04:37.480 --> 01:04:40.560
Serhan Dagtas: It's going to be reviewed by a fully convened board.

554
01:04:40.930 --> 01:04:46.410
Serhan Dagtas: The study approval is usually for one year, although radiation studies are approved for six months.

555
01:04:46.550 --> 01:04:48.800
Serhan Dagtas: And it has a continuing review.

556
01:04:49.260 --> 01:04:55.349
Serhan Dagtas: Full board studies will have to complete the continuing review application in Pools IRP.

557
01:04:56.010 --> 01:05:04.829
Serhan Dagtas: I ask that you take a look at those IRB approval letters, because it's going to have study-specific guidelines for you, as far as your approval and your continuing review.

558
01:05:05.560 --> 01:05:21.289
Serhan Dagtas: Examples of full board would be studies involving vulnerable populations, such as children or prisoners, novel therapeutic interventions, or collecting information that could place a participant at risk of civil or criminal liability, or may cause other societal harms.

559
01:05:24.070 --> 01:05:34.939
Serhan Dagtas: Okay, remember, if your research is determined to be exempt, you will receive an IRB approval letter, and then your application is going to be closed in full IRB.

560
01:05:35.110 --> 01:05:44.620
Serhan Dagtas: Now, only your application is closed. Remember, your study is still active, and you're required to perform your study as you indicated in your study documents.

