935
02:00:34.520 --> 02:00:43.910
Serhan Dagtas: So, if you're using technical jargon, you need to tell us in more ordinary language what it is that you mean.

936
02:00:44.010 --> 02:01:02.800
Serhan Dagtas: And if you use abbreviations, please define those abbreviations, because it's easy to get lost in abbreviations if you're not familiar with them. So give us an anchor for both the jargon and for any of the abbreviations that you use.

937
02:01:05.090 --> 02:01:12.199
Serhan Dagtas: As we move to the second question, describe the methods and procedures.

938
02:01:12.450 --> 02:01:23.689
Serhan Dagtas: Here what you're going to be telling us is what is it that you expect the participants to do? What is their experience going to be?

939
02:01:23.690 --> 02:01:44.770
Serhan Dagtas: So, if you're asking them to exercise with electrodes attached, if you're asking them to take a survey, if you're asking them to, answer interview questions, if you're showing them things, images, and getting responses, we need to know that, and this is where you tell us that information.

940
02:01:44.790 --> 02:01:55.440
Serhan Dagtas: We also need to know how you plan to record that information. So, are you videoing? Are you audioing? Or, excuse me, doing an audio recording?

941
02:01:55.920 --> 02:02:01.029
Serhan Dagtas: Are they answering an electronic survey? Is it a paper survey? Does the…

942
02:02:01.140 --> 02:02:09.449
Serhan Dagtas: Researcher take notes, whatever it is that you're using to collect information, please include that here.

943
02:02:09.590 --> 02:02:15.719
Serhan Dagtas: Also remember that participation in your study is voluntary.

944
02:02:16.010 --> 02:02:28.849
Serhan Dagtas: So not only does that mean people have the right to decide whether to participate or not at the beginning, it means they have the right all the way through the study.

945
02:02:28.980 --> 02:02:39.080
Serhan Dagtas: So, they can skip questions, and they can withdraw at any time. We will want this information not only in the consent form.

946
02:02:39.200 --> 02:02:46.370
Serhan Dagtas: But we'll also want some information in the answer to question 2 about what happens to the data.

947
02:02:46.520 --> 02:03:04.560
Serhan Dagtas: If a participant withdraws. Now, I'll be the first to tell you that missing data is the bane of every researcher's experience. It's not easy to deal with, but we have to let participants withdraw. So, what are you going to do? Are you going to throw out their data? Are you going to use it?

948
02:03:04.860 --> 02:03:17.100
Serhan Dagtas: Whatever your decision is, you need to tell the participants up front, so we do expect that not only here, in the answer to this question, but we also expect to see it in the consent form.

949
02:03:18.040 --> 02:03:21.130
Serhan Dagtas: If you're collecting sensitive data.

950
02:03:21.380 --> 02:03:40.639
Serhan Dagtas: or if there's safety issues, or if there's deception, then we also expect some details here about how any negative effects from those, circumstances will be mitigated. So, you know, how are you kind of accommodating for that, or taking that into account?

951
02:03:40.640 --> 02:03:43.910
Serhan Dagtas: And on the procedures that you follow.

952
02:03:44.380 --> 02:03:49.419
Serhan Dagtas: Remember that if you're collecting or analyzing information about people.

953
02:03:50.440 --> 02:03:57.199
Serhan Dagtas: Even if you didn't directly ask them for it, this can still be considered human participants research.

954
02:03:57.620 --> 02:04:06.420
Serhan Dagtas: In addition, we expect you to give a realistic time estimate for how long this is going to take to participate.

955
02:04:06.420 --> 02:04:18.100
Serhan Dagtas: This also has to be in the consent form, but give that information to us here as well. It'll make a lot of sense to the reviewers if it's in the context of what you're asking the participant to do.

956
02:04:18.460 --> 02:04:27.229
Serhan Dagtas: We want to know how many participants you need. So just tell us what your goal is in terms of the number.

957
02:04:27.330 --> 02:04:34.180
Serhan Dagtas: And then, one of the things we ask is, will you send follow-ups or reminders?

958
02:04:34.930 --> 02:04:36.879
Serhan Dagtas: You can say yes or no.

959
02:04:37.450 --> 02:04:45.110
Serhan Dagtas: If you say no, you can't send reminders. So, if you say no here, and later you find

960
02:04:45.510 --> 02:04:52.829
Serhan Dagtas: And this is a common experience, you know, not as many people signed up as you thought they would and you want to send a reminder, well, you can't.

961
02:04:53.150 --> 02:05:08.400
Serhan Dagtas: What you have to do then is submit a modification to us because you told us you weren't sending reminders. So think carefully about whether you might want to send reminders when you first design all of this. We need to see copies.

962
02:05:08.400 --> 02:05:18.099
Serhan Dagtas: of everything that the participants see. So, if you, again, going up to the partner, if you, you know, you show them things like images.

963
02:05:18.100 --> 02:05:31.939
Serhan Dagtas: Anything they see, we want to see a copy of it. All the reminders, we need to see a copy of. So, those will all be attached at the end of the protocol, and we'll be, you know, we'll expect to see them at that point.

964
02:05:33.940 --> 02:05:38.090
Serhan Dagtas: Okay, question 3?

965
02:05:39.100 --> 02:05:41.140
Serhan Dagtas: As we're moving to that one…

966
02:05:41.550 --> 02:06:04.759
Serhan Dagtas: This is a question about the recruiting procedures. And again, just as a reminder, we'll need to see copies of everything at the end. And that means basically, if you have a script for how they will recruit, a flyer, whatever, an email, we need to see all of that at the end because we're looking basically for what you're saying.

967
02:06:04.760 --> 02:06:11.660
Serhan Dagtas: I think you've covered some information about the study in an appropriate fashion.

968
02:06:12.040 --> 02:06:16.180
Serhan Dagtas: So, one of the questions for recruiting is who?

969
02:06:16.410 --> 02:06:20.089
Serhan Dagtas: Who are you going to ask to participate in this study?

970
02:06:20.410 --> 02:06:33.570
Serhan Dagtas: This is probably something or should be something you've already addressed for us to a certain extent in that first question when you told us about the purpose of the study and the research questions.

971
02:06:33.570 --> 02:06:47.070
Serhan Dagtas: So we do want to know what their characteristics are. So, you know, sometimes people have just a very broad swath that they're asking to participate. It might be anybody over 18, and there's nothing else to it.

972
02:06:47.150 --> 02:06:54.620
Serhan Dagtas: But in a lot of cases, people want participants with certain characteristics, so they might want

973
02:06:54.750 --> 02:07:11.039
Serhan Dagtas: participants who are healthy, and so they're not… they'll only include healthy people. So we want to know that, and we want to know why. So give us a research-based, scientific explanation for why it is you're focused on this group.

974
02:07:11.080 --> 02:07:16.639
Serhan Dagtas: And, the other groups are not going to be asked to participate.

975
02:07:16.820 --> 02:07:28.619
Serhan Dagtas: If you're focused on middle income, tell us why again. We're going to look at that in the context of the research. You know, whatever it is, whatever special characteristics people might have.

976
02:07:28.620 --> 02:07:37.919
Serhan Dagtas: that you have chosen to focus in on the research needs to be tied to your research questions. And again, we want that explanation.

977
02:07:37.920 --> 02:07:42.079
Serhan Dagtas: So we can understand what's going on and evaluate it. Sure.

978
02:07:42.320 --> 02:07:54.840
Serhan Dagtas: Age can be an important factor. We're focusing in today's presentation primarily on adult studies, and that's because most of the studies that people do are with adults.

979
02:07:55.650 --> 02:08:15.210
Serhan Dagtas: In the case of children, there are some special procedures. Again, we're not covering that today. But one of the questions is whether the participants are 18 or older, because that means they can give their informed consent. Now, so you're going to have to tell us how you will verify that.

980
02:08:15.400 --> 02:08:27.120
Serhan Dagtas: Often that can be a very simple procedure, where you put a line in at the bottom of the consent form, and a checkbox, the person verifies that they're 18 or older.

981
02:08:27.350 --> 02:08:33.980
Serhan Dagtas: And, then you have that documentation, or that acknowledgement, depending on how you're getting consent.

982
02:08:34.160 --> 02:08:48.320
Serhan Dagtas: Again, there's special procedures for those who are under 18. For those who are under 18, you would need parental permission and an asset form, so there's more to it, than what I'm covering right now.

983
02:08:49.650 --> 02:08:58.190
Serhan Dagtas: With recruiting, we also want to know, basically, how are you approaching people? How are you getting to them? So, is this by…

984
02:08:58.210 --> 02:09:11.979
Serhan Dagtas: email, it's by a flyer that you're posting, some kind of announcement. There are all kinds of different ways to do this. We, of course, want to know what you're going to tell them.

985
02:09:12.240 --> 02:09:25.719
Serhan Dagtas: And one of the things we're going to be looking at is, is this something, you know, that is an appropriate way to approach people? And, you know, how does it match with the characteristics that you've given us above?

986
02:09:26.050 --> 02:09:27.270
Serhan Dagtas: So…

987
02:09:27.270 --> 02:09:46.799
Serhan Dagtas: A lot of times, people will need contact information, so they have… they know what they want to say, they want… they know they're going to get the information to them in a certain way, but they may need the names and contact information, you know, the participants in advance. If this is the case, you need to tell us how you're going to get that information, and we're going to look to see

988
02:09:46.800 --> 02:09:55.359
Serhan Dagtas: whether you have permission for that. So, things to keep in mind, especially in a university context, are there VRBO requirements?

989
02:09:55.620 --> 02:10:13.409
Serhan Dagtas: So, that is a responsibility that everyone has within a university context. If you're recruiting outside the university, we'll still ask how you're getting that information. So, if someone's going to give you a list of names and contact information.

990
02:10:13.410 --> 02:10:16.529
Serhan Dagtas: Do they have the authority to give you that list?

991
02:10:16.920 --> 02:10:25.859
Serhan Dagtas: If you're the researcher, you can't give yourself authority. So we're going to ask for somebody above you to give that, permission.

992
02:10:27.780 --> 02:10:35.639
Serhan Dagtas: If you're a student, we want to see the signed permission letter from that person granting you permission. If you're a faculty member.

993
02:10:35.760 --> 02:10:41.519
Serhan Dagtas: We won't require it to be signed before the approval, but we want to see the permission letter.

994
02:10:42.040 --> 02:10:49.470
Serhan Dagtas: And then it's on you. So, if something happens later, and you didn't get that sign, and somebody said, well, they didn't have access.

995
02:10:50.290 --> 02:11:05.279
Serhan Dagtas: that's a problem, but we want you to know that you do need to have permission. Now, sometimes information's out there on a public website, whatever. Tell us that. We just need to know the details so we can understand

996
02:11:05.520 --> 02:11:11.059
Serhan Dagtas: What's going on? And again, we just need to see copies of everything that you're doing.

997
02:11:12.600 --> 02:11:13.410
Serhan Dagtas: Okay.

998
02:11:14.420 --> 02:11:15.660
Serhan Dagtas: Relax.

999
02:11:15.880 --> 02:11:20.150
Serhan Dagtas: go to… Question 4…

1000
02:11:20.690 --> 02:11:37.160
Serhan Dagtas: Which is compensation. And not everybody has compensation in their studies. So some of you can just, you know, NA through this because you're not doing this. But we have a fair number of studies that do use compensation.

1001
02:11:37.740 --> 02:11:45.070
Serhan Dagtas: So if you're using compensation, of course, tell us that you are, but then we want to know more details.

1002
02:11:45.190 --> 02:11:51.350
Serhan Dagtas: So what compensation are you giving people? Is it going to be extra credit?

1003
02:11:51.500 --> 02:11:57.629
Serhan Dagtas: Is it a gift card? Money? Are you entering someone in a lottery?

1004
02:11:57.780 --> 02:11:59.700
Serhan Dagtas: And we want to know how much.

1005
02:12:00.400 --> 02:12:03.290
Serhan Dagtas: How much money? How much extra credit?

1006
02:12:03.750 --> 02:12:15.289
Serhan Dagtas: And one of the things to recognize is that compensation is an incentive. So it can be a mild inducement to do the study.

1007
02:12:15.520 --> 02:12:20.639
Serhan Dagtas: Or, it can be a compensation for the resources that somebody uses

1008
02:12:20.980 --> 02:12:35.759
Serhan Dagtas: to get to the study, or the fact that they're spending time with you, and they're not, you know, being paid for their regular job, or whatever, so you can compensate them in that way. But compensation is not a benefit of the study.

1009
02:12:36.070 --> 02:12:50.129
Serhan Dagtas: So, when we get later to the benefits section, that's not one of the benefits, and you don't list it on the informed consent form as a benefit either, although you do have to talk about the compensation on the informed consent.

1010
02:12:50.380 --> 02:13:01.820
Serhan Dagtas: What the IRB is interested in knowing, and you're going to need to give us the details for this, is whether the incentive or compensation that you are offering

1011
02:13:02.130 --> 02:13:10.559
Serhan Dagtas: is… Reasonable given what is required in the study and who the participants are.

1012
02:13:10.700 --> 02:13:20.970
Serhan Dagtas: So, we're looking to see whether there's a coercive effect. So, is this incentive for compensation so much

1013
02:13:21.240 --> 02:13:28.840
Serhan Dagtas: That people would put their needs or their safety aside to participate in your study.

1014
02:13:29.160 --> 02:13:36.100
Serhan Dagtas: So those are, we're looking for that balance. The balance between, you know, is there a reasonable amount?

1015
02:13:36.310 --> 02:13:47.239
Serhan Dagtas: that a person would like to have, but can easily walk away from, versus, oh, I really need this, and I don't want to do it, but I'm going to do it and take it and conquer it.

1016
02:13:47.450 --> 02:13:52.079
Serhan Dagtas: Okay, I'm going to stop this video here.

1017
